# PROJECT: AI-Powered Industrial Safety Monitoring, PPE Detection & Business Intelligence Platform

Act as a senior full-stack software engineer, computer vision engineer, UI/UX designer, database architect, and AI engineer. Build a complete, functional, responsive web application for industrial workplace safety monitoring using YOLOv8, Python, Flask, OpenCV, a database, and interactive Power BI-style analytics dashboards.

Do not create only a landing page, UI mockup, or static prototype. Build a working end-to-end application with a functional backend, real file uploads, database persistence, actual AI inference integration, and dynamically generated reports.

## 1. PROJECT OBJECTIVE

Develop an AI-powered safety monitoring platform that allows an authorized industry manager or administrator to upload workplace images and videos from:

- Construction sites
- Automobile manufacturing factories
- Automobile assembly lines
- Industrial production units
- Warehouses and logistics centers
- Other field-work environments

The system must analyze uploaded media to identify workers, check the presence or absence of required Personal Protective Equipment (PPE), identify possible safety violations, calculate transparent safety and risk scores, generate immediate in-application alerts, and display interactive analytical dashboards.

Examples:
- A worker wearing a safety helmet, reflective safety vest, gloves, and safety shoes should be evaluated against the configured safety policy.
- A worker without a helmet should generate a missing-helmet alert if the model can reliably identify that condition.
- A worker without a reflective safety vest should generate a missing-vest alert.
- Missing gloves should be flagged when glove detection is supported by the configured model and image quality.
- Different workers in the same image must be evaluated individually whenever the model can reliably distinguish them.
- Different images and videos must produce different reports based on their actual detection results.

Never report a PPE violation as confirmed when the model has not actually detected enough evidence. Provide an “Unknown / Unable to verify” state when visibility, occlusion, or model limitations prevent a reliable decision.

